00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.060
on top of Kubernetes
definitely could get better,

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.905
and we'd like to have
Donna tell us about that.

00:03:57.905 --> 00:04:00.910
DONNA MALAYERI: All right,
so our solution to this

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:01.900
is Cloud Run.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.450
This is the new product that
we've launched this week,

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:07.510
and we're bringing
serverless to containers.

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:09.820
So what does that mean?

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:11.440
Now serverless is
an interesting term,

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:14.470
because of course it's a
misnomer-- there are obviously

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:15.940
servers involved.

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:18.670
It's also an interesting term
when you talk about a cluster

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:20.510
that you manage yourself.

00:04:20.510 --> 00:04:23.470
So what we talk about is
a serverless developer

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:24.460
experience.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:27.370
If you use the fully hosted
Cloud Run, which is the default

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:33.023
one if you go into the Cloud Run
console and create a service,

00:04:33.023 --> 00:04:33.940
it's fully serverless.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:37.630
There are no clus-- there's
no cluster to manage.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:39.130
And you only pay
for what you use.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:42.880
Essentially, we, Google
Cloud, is your operator.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.550
We're managing all of that
underlying infrastructure

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:46.510
for you.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:51.790
Now, the reality is that
when you have a cloud vendor

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:54.220
as your operator, there
is a lot of assumptions

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:55.970
that we make that may
not suit your needs,

00:04:55.970 --> 00:04:59.980
and that's where Kubernetes
comes in and Cloud Run on GKE.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:03.640
And what this means is if
you use Cloud Run on GKE,

00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:07.450
your operators have this
great Kubernetes experience.

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:10.840
You can set up role-based access
control, networking policies,

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.390
you can decide what cluster you
want, all that kind of stuff.

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:17.020
But developers are
abstracted from this,

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:21.420
and they have a serverless
developer experience

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.430
that can scale up and down.

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:29.485
So Cloud Run on GKE compared
to the fully hosted Cloud Run

00:05:29.485 --> 00:05:30.860
gives you a lot
more flexibility.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:32.235
Of course there's
more to manage,

00:05:32.235 --> 00:05:35.208
but if that's what you're
already intending to do,

00:05:35.208 --> 00:05:36.250
it can be a great choice.

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:40.400
If you already have GKE, Cloud
Run on GKE is a great option.

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.630
So you have more machine
options, you can run on GPUs,

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:47.050
you can access more things
that are currently integrated

00:05:47.050 --> 00:05:48.310
with Cloud Run on GKE.

00:05:54.045 --> 00:05:54.920
MARK CHMARNY: Thanks.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:58.430
So Donna has explained kind of
the differences between the two

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:00.830
flavors of Cloud Run.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:03.410
Cloud Run gives you the
consistent developer experience

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:05.780
regardless where you run it,
whether it's on a managed

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:09.230
service or on Cloud Run on GKE.

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:12.050
But coming back to
the original picture

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:17.000
that we talked about, when
developers use Cloud Run, some

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.965
of the things that they had to
do before are still happening.

00:06:19.965 --> 00:06:21.590
They're just happening
behind the scene

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:25.110
and happen automatically,
or they're made much easier.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.717
So you continue getting the
very same power of Kubernetes

00:06:27.717 --> 00:06:29.300
underneath, but
you're able to benefit

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:32.690
from a lot of automation
that Cloud Run on GKE

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:33.900
delivers to you.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.080
And what that means
is that you don't

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:39.680
have to focus as much on
the manual step, the shaving

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:42.680
the axe, so to speak, over and
over building those images,

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.500
there's a way to
integrate other--

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.400
exposing the service
to the internet.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.120
And because Cloud Run is
supporting Knative APIs--

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:52.670
and more about that
later, you will

00:06:52.670 --> 00:06:56.000
be able to preserve that
same developer experience

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.960
whether you run on Cloud Run
on GKE, Cloud Run hosted,

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:03.290
or whether you run on top of
another Knative-compatible

00:07:03.290 --> 00:07:05.870
offering, whether
it's on-premise

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:08.270
that you run yourself,
or whether it's consumed

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:10.610
through another provider.

00:07:10.610 --> 00:07:14.510
So let's go a little deeper
into some of the benefits

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:17.600
the developer gets from
running Cloud Run on GKE

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:19.920
and what that means to them.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:22.310
So first of all, I was
talking about automation.

00:07:22.310 --> 00:07:25.730
There's one of the
key features that we

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:27.870
talk about with
regards to Cloud Run,

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:31.442
whether it's on GKE or on
Knative, is auto scaling.

00:07:31.442 --> 00:07:32.900
Which means that
you as a developer

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:34.520
can write your
application and be

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:37.640
able to respond to
unpredictable usage patterns.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:39.860
Whether it's a disaster
that suddenly creates

00:07:39.860 --> 00:07:43.580
lots more demand
on your service,

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:45.750
or whether Justin Bieber
tweeted about some jeans

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:49.670
and your marketing application
has to respond to some service

00:07:49.670 --> 00:07:51.200
much more--

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:55.880
or be able to scale much
larger audience of visitors.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:07:58.640
What it also means that
Cloud Run manages your code

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:00.650
and configuration as unit.

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:03.490
The benefit of that is
that you have at all point

00:08:03.490 --> 00:08:06.170
the ability to go back
to the last known state.

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:08.370
So it's not just the
code that was compiled

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:11.150
that's a certain version plus
a different configuration

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:13.170
and suddenly you have to
change the differences,

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:15.230
it's a unit, that
snapshot whenever

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:19.410
you apply a change to a service
that treats them together.

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:22.340
The other thing is that
Cloud Run underneath--

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:26.390
Cloud Run on GKE underneath is
using Istio, a very powerful

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:31.070
mesh service, that in order to
make that accessible to you,

00:08:31.070 --> 00:08:33.409
we expose a very easy
to reason about object

00:08:33.409 --> 00:08:36.679
model that allows you to
deal with things like routes

00:08:36.679 --> 00:08:39.230
management or observability
around metrics

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:40.620
that you want to capture.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:44.373
So in a situation of a-- a very
common situation, for example,

00:08:44.373 --> 00:08:46.040
when you come to a
conference like this,

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:48.440
you get excited about
particularly technology,

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:51.650
you deploy your
application, it runs now.

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:53.690
You have users, great.

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:55.670
And now you say, I
want to run a change--

00:08:55.670 --> 00:08:57.715
I mean, if it was me, I would
probably have a bug in there

00:08:57.715 --> 00:08:59.798
so I have to fix it, but
you write a perfect code,

00:08:59.798 --> 00:09:01.820
so no problem there.

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:04.230
You want to make a change,
but you have active users,

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:05.700
so how do you do this?

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:09.590
So the ability to manage Istio
underneath and manage those

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:12.920
routes reliably allows you to
support things like blue-green

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:16.070
deployment where you have a
new version of application

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:18.590
or experiments which you
can actually start sending

00:09:18.590 --> 00:09:20.390
incrementally larger number of--

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:23.190
percentage of traffic
to this new version.

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:28.670
And on top of this also, Cloud
Run on GKE is also plugable.

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:30.980
What that means is
that it connects

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:35.270
very well with a series of
native services offered by GCP.

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:37.610
Cloud Run or Stackdriver,
whatever it may be--

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:39.780
and Donna has listed a
few other ones there.

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:42.320
But it also is extendable
by our partners,

00:09:42.320 --> 00:09:44.640
and there's number
of them out there.

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:48.380
The ones I want to highlight
here is GitLab and Datadog.

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:50.060
If you've been in
developer space,

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:52.510
you probably are aware
of those companies.

00:09:54.998 --> 00:09:57.040
