can't get through with cars.

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:05.820
So to have some sort
of data available

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:08.710
allows the first responders
to know what's going on.

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:12.682
So in this case, there was just
a little tropical depression

00:12:12.682 --> 00:12:14.640
on the 7th and 8th, and
then all of a sudden it

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:17.880
became a huge storm on the 9th.

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:21.930
It crashed into Mexico Beach
on the 10th, and on the 11th

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:25.710
we had data inside our portal
for our clients to use.

00:12:25.710 --> 00:12:28.440
So it's very impressive
how we can get data

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:31.980
from a satellite into our system
and in front of our clients

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:34.170
in a very quick fashion.

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:36.300
This is actually very
important for our clients.

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.633
If we have a client that's
an insurance company, if they

00:12:38.633 --> 00:12:40.678
see that picture
right there, they

00:12:40.678 --> 00:12:41.970
don't have to send someone out.

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:44.173
They can just cut checks.

00:12:44.173 --> 00:12:45.840
And this is very
important to the people

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:47.070
that were in these homes.

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:49.660
They can start their
lives a lot quicker.

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:52.692
It also like saves a lot of
money for the insurance company

00:12:52.692 --> 00:12:54.150
because they don't
need to send out

00:12:54.150 --> 00:12:56.610
people which can
cost $6,000 to $8,000

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:58.480
just to send out
a single person.

00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:02.970
So helps people in
real life, and it

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.950
does some good for people.

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:06.960
So I'm going to
show how we do this.

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:12.450
So this is our spatial
data processing pipeline.

00:13:12.450 --> 00:13:16.310
So starting on the left we
have raw data coming in,

00:13:16.310 --> 00:13:19.290
and all the way to the right
we have our web browser.

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:21.290
Each of those arrows is
a program run by someone

00:13:21.290 --> 00:13:23.900
like me wrapped into
a Docker container

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:27.470
with lots of good code in an OS,
and then on top of that a ton

00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:32.640
of YAML so we can send it
up to the Google Cloud.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:34.390
I actually joke that
I'm a YAML programmer

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:37.000
at this point because I'm
sure most of my lines of code

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:43.180
are actually YAML, but that's
the way it has worked for us.

00:13:43.180 --> 00:13:46.000
It's a pretty good system,
but as you can see,

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:47.000
it's pretty complicated.

00:13:47.000 --> 00:13:49.500
I'll just walk you through what
happened with the satellite.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:51.430
So that would be
at the bottom here.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:53.080
If we get satellite data in--

00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:54.480
these are crazy data sets.

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:57.522
They're 80,000 pixels
by 40,000 pixels.

00:13:57.522 --> 00:13:59.230
And we don't have to
process one of them,

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:02.180
we have to process
tons of them at a time.

00:14:02.180 --> 00:14:05.180
So it'll be lots
of them coming in.

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:07.960
And then we load those
into Google Cloud Storage,

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:11.370
and we have to process
those into images

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:15.060
that are things that our
map server can serve up

00:14:15.060 --> 00:14:16.810
so our clients can see it.

00:14:16.810 --> 00:14:22.470
So there's lots of steps to get
our data from our source all

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:26.140
the way to the browser.

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:29.580
It's not terrible, but like this
is the system we were using.

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:32.190
At the top, that's vector
data, so that would be

00:14:32.190 --> 00:14:34.273
stuff like parcels and whatnot.

00:14:34.273 --> 00:14:35.940
So I'm going to show
the way that we can

00:14:35.940 --> 00:14:37.600
improve this with Cloud Run.

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:40.620
So this is replacing all
of the image processing.

00:14:40.620 --> 00:14:42.570
We don't need all those
intermediary files

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:46.860
because we can
scale down and up.

00:14:46.860 --> 00:14:50.130
Very simply, we can skip
all the pre-processing,

00:14:50.130 --> 00:14:52.830
serve off of an individual file.

00:14:52.830 --> 00:14:54.960
So we have one TIFF and
we can use that TIFF

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:57.420
for all of our machine
learning, or we

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:00.090
can use that for all of
our spectral analysis,

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:03.030
and we can use that
for our map server.

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:04.800
So this makes our
data lot simpler.

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:06.800
Like, this is the way
that we think of our data.

00:15:06.800 --> 00:15:08.175
It's a satellite
capture, we have

00:15:08.175 --> 00:15:10.100
one file we store on
Google Cloud Storage,

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:14.637
and from there we can do all the
things we need to do with it.

00:15:14.637 --> 00:15:16.970
This is very easy for us to
implement because we already

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:18.680
have everything as
Docker, so we can just

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:22.750
basically send that Docker image
up that Cloud Run and it works.

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:24.500
If we do want to try
out a new deployment,

00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:27.710
we can do a blue-green,
so that's fantastic.

00:15:27.710 --> 00:15:29.780
So, I mean, this
is a lot simpler

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:33.783
than that previous slide,
and I like it a lot.

00:15:33.783 --> 00:15:34.700
MARK CHMARNY: Show us.

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:36.408
MIKE MOMMSEN: I'm
going to show you guys.

00:15:39.260 --> 00:15:41.330
All right, so this is
a demo of that map tile

00:15:41.330 --> 00:15:43.550
that I was describing.

00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:46.445
So this is actually
running on Cloud Run.

00:15:46.445 --> 00:15:48.320
And you saw those tiles
as they were loading.

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:50.750
Each one of those
tiles is a request that

00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:52.850
goes to the Cloud Run server.

00:15:52.850 --> 00:15:57.950
That Cloud Run server
sends a request to GCS.

00:15:57.950 --> 00:16:01.420
And then it gets the data back
and then it recompiles it,

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:03.172
and it makes a tile
that we can see.

00:16:03.172 --> 00:16:04.630
MARK CHMARNY: Donna
has a question.

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:06.505
DONNA MALAYERI: So this
might be good for you

00:16:06.505 --> 00:16:08.388
because you're a
geospatial engineer,

00:16:08.388 --> 00:16:09.430
but I can't see anything.

00:16:09.430 --> 00:16:12.843
Can you please
enlarge and enhance?

00:16:12.843 --> 00:16:14.260
MIKE MOMMSEN: So
this isn't "CSI,"

00:16:14.260 --> 00:16:16.910
so we can't do the enhancing.

00:16:16.910 --> 00:16:17.425
But--

00:16:17.425 --> 00:16:18.425
MARK CHMARNY: Real-time?

00:16:18.425 --> 00:16:18.925
No.

00:16:18.925 --> 00:16:19.570
[LAUGHS]

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:23.080
MIKE MOMMSEN: But
actually, because we

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:24.580
didn't have to
pre-process the image

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:26.620
and we could serve
straight off that TIFF,

00:16:26.620 --> 00:16:28.360
that TIFF has a lot
more colors in it,

00:16:28.360 --> 00:16:30.110
and we have to reduce
the amount of colors

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:31.207
that go to the browser.

00:16:31.207 --> 00:16:33.040
So because we didn't
have to pre-process it,

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:36.470
we can actually change the
way that we show the colors.

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:40.930
So this is that same image
that I was showing over here,

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:43.150
but we did a stretch to it.

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:46.030
So previously, if we would
have messed up the stretch,

00:16:46.030 --> 00:16:47.630
and this does
happen, we would've

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:49.240
had a dark image
or a light image.

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:51.760
But now we can expose
to our front end

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:56.200
or to our clients' ability
to change the way that they

00:16:56.200 --> 00:16:57.040
view the image.

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:01.900
So I can get in here
and I can actually

00:17:01.900 --> 00:17:03.280
make this image look way worse.

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:07.030
So rather than doing a stretch
that goes between 2% and 98%,

00:17:07.030 --> 00:17:10.579
I'll go from 2% to 70%.

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:13.410
And it's washed out
and looks terrible.

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:16.563
But it shows that we can
do on-the-fly processing,

00:17:16.563 --> 00:17:18.230
and it's going to
change the way that we

00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:23.369
can use our whole spatial
data processing pipeline.

00:17:23.369 --> 00:17:27.150
And I'm very excited to
take this and apply it

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:31.020
to all the other areas that
you saw in that previous slide.

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:31.916
Thanks, guys.

00:17:31.916 --> 00:17:33.410
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:34.410
DONNA MALAYERI: Awesome.

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:37.110
Thanks, Mike.

00:17:37.110 --> 00:17:39.780
What I really like
about this demo

00:17:39.780 --> 00:17:42.480
is that you didn't have to--

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:44.230
you can move away
from batch processing,

00:17:44.230 --> 00:17:47.340
but you also don't have to
design for peak capacity,

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:50.670
because you can't really
plan for a natural disaster.

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:53.100
And until-- if you're not using
something like serverless,

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:56.010
you would have to provision
a lot of extra machines.

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:59.820
But here, when customers
want this data, this image

00:17:59.820 --> 00:18:02.090
data on demand, you
can do the scaling,

00:18:02.090 --> 00:18:04.590
and that's really the power of
having a serverless developer

00:18:04.590 --> 00:18:06.130
experience.

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:12.640
So if we go back to
the slides, all right.

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:16.720
So Mark, in this case,
Mike had a container image.

00:18:16.720 --> 00:18:20.590
Can you show us what happens
if you only have source?

00:18:20.590 --> 00:18:21.700
MARK CHMARNY: Good.

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:24.740
I guess we--

00:18:24.740 --> 00:18:27.495
I'm going to do a quick
overview here maybe first.

00:18:27.495 --> 00:18:29.120
Can you go back to
slides for a second?

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:34.197
So I can do the demo
in a second here,

00:18:34.197 --> 00:18:36.030
but I want to just kind
of walk you through.

00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:38.700
So a lot of times we talked
about developers just wanting

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:39.900
to write code.

00:18:39.900 --> 00:18:44.190
So in kind of a
GitOps-like setup or use

00:18:44.190 --> 00:18:46.440
case, what that means is the
developers are committing

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:49.830
their code on an ongoing
basis into code repository,

00:18:49.830 --> 00:18:52.560
and there are a series of
unit tests on their machines

00:18:52.560 --> 00:18:55.380
and there are integration
tests that they're running

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:58.650
against the code repositories,
so whenever a new commit comes

00:18:58.650 --> 00:19:00.360
in, it's executing.

00:19:00.360 --> 00:19:03.540
But sooner or later,
the operator-- sorry,

00:19:03.540 --> 00:19:06.250
the release manager will
come in and say, OK, I'm

00:19:06.250 --> 00:19:09.140
ready to make a new cat and
deploy a new deployment.

00:19:09.140 --> 00:19:12.550
So they will come in, they
will create a trigger in--

00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:14.550
what we're going to show
here is the integration

00:19:14.550 --> 00:19:18.330
of Cloud Build, which is another
hosted offering from Google

00:19:18.330 --> 00:19:20.992
that's managing
the build process.

00:19:20.992 --> 00:19:22.950
We're going to create a
trigger in there that's

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:24.750
going to go through
the series of steps

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:26.510
and deploy a new
image to Cloud Run.

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:29.122
So 100% transparently,
no user involved.

00:19:29.122 --> 00:19:30.330
All right, let's switch back.

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:38.880
So what I have here is a clearly
for demo design application.

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:41.310
Unless you're in a prime
number-calculating business--

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:43.530
and who wouldn't
want to be in that?

00:19:43.530 --> 00:19:44.730
That's kind of all it does.

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:48.450
You give it a number
and it calculates--

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:52.010
I don't know, let's do
something different.

00:19:52.010 --> 00:19:52.538
Great.

00:19:52.538 --> 00:19:53.330
So we get a number.

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:55.340
It's really not-- the
application is really not

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:57.540
important here, please
don't focus on that.

00:19:57.540 --> 00:19:59.490
What's interesting here,
a couple of things.

00:19:59.490 --> 00:20:01.640
First of all, we use this
also as a REST service.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:05.960
So you can use it to test
the scalability use cases,

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:07.790
because you can pass
a larger number, which

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:10.580
will consume more CPU, but
also not something we're

00:20:10.580 --> 00:20:12.390
going to do today.

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:14.370
The part I want to
focus on is over here.

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:17.210
What do we have is
for demo purposes,

00:20:17.210 --> 00:20:19.880
we're injecting the
release tag that

00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:23.330
was a signed by the release
manager in the repository

00:20:23.330 --> 00:20:25.670
so that we can-- when this
entire application travels

00:20:25.670 --> 00:20:27.045
through the pipeline
I showed you

00:20:27.045 --> 00:20:28.830
before, we can actually see it.

00:20:28.830 --> 00:20:32.380
So let's take a look
at this application.

00:20:32.380 --> 00:20:35.042
This application-- again,
the application purpose

00:20:35.042 --> 00:20:36.000
is really not relevant.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:38.280
What we're going to focus on
is the release manager process.

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:39.840
There was 46
releases, you can see

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:42.438
we've been busy testing this.

00:20:42.438 --> 00:20:43.980
So I asked the
release manager, going

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:45.450
to just create a new release.

00:20:45.450 --> 00:20:46.200
And you know what?

00:20:46.200 --> 00:20:48.987
I mean, let's call
this something like a--

00:20:48.987 --> 00:20:50.070
DONNA MALAYERI: Next Demo.

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:51.070
MARK CHMARNY: Next Demo.

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:52.590
That's a great idea.

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:56.080
And Next Demo.

00:20:56.080 --> 00:21:04.040
And because this is
demo, hopefully you

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:07.720
do better release notes than I
do in your production system.

00:21:07.720 --> 00:21:11.610
And when we fire
this, what actually

00:21:11.610 --> 00:21:14.430
is happening here
is that Cloud Run--

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:16.570
sorry, Cloud Build
automatically notices this.

00:21:16.570 --> 00:21:18.960
So let's go kind of
deeper into this process.

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:23.100
What we have here is
information about the status

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:26.670
of that particular build,
including the repository

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:29.310
that it came from,
but it also has

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:31.375
a trigger that we can
kind of interrogate.

00:21:31.375 --> 00:21:33.250
So what happens here is
the trigger's set up,

00:21:33.250 --> 00:21:35.280
it indicates where
the repository

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:39.290
was-- so in this case it was
the GitHub repo that we went to.

00:21:39.290 --> 00:21:41.430
Had some name that you
want to be able to use.

00:21:41.430 --> 00:21:43.347
There's two different
ways you can trigger it.

00:21:43.347 --> 00:21:45.930
So you can triggered
on a branch created

00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:49.900
for QA/QC kind of
purposes, but in this case,

00:21:49.900 --> 00:21:51.330
we went with a tag.

00:21:51.330 --> 00:21:54.750
And the tag has a regex-- in
this case, release-v. Anything

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:58.650
after that, whenever Cloud
Build sees one of the stacks,

00:21:58.650 --> 00:22:00.240
it will build the
entire process.

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:02.260
And there is a
configuration file,

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:04.170
but let's go over here quickly--

00:22:04.170 --> 00:22:06.780
oh.

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:08.737
History.

00:22:08.737 --> 00:22:09.820
Let's take a look at this.

00:22:09.820 --> 00:22:12.960
So this build configuration
has number of steps,

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:14.370
and those are defined by you.

00:22:14.370 --> 00:22:19.290
So you can build simple
only test, you can run--

00:22:19.290 --> 00:22:21.540
build process and
push a repository.

00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:24.660
But what we're doing in here is
we're capping the entire source

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:27.390
code, we're running a
test, we're creating--

00:22:27.390 --> 00:22:30.870
building an image, publishing
that image to the repository,

00:22:30.870 --> 00:22:32.783
GC image repository.

00:22:32.783 --> 00:22:34.200
We're creating a
manifest in order

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:35.520
so we can inject
that environment

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:36.900
variable and other
configurations

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:38.490
that you need and deploy it.

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:41.370
And obviously there is a log
file you can access this.

00:22:41.370 --> 00:22:43.290
So hopefully, if everything
worked correctly,

00:22:43.290 --> 00:22:47.550
we can come in here, refresh it,
and you see my next demo label.

00:22:47.550 --> 00:22:49.040
This happens transactionally.

00:22:49.040 --> 00:22:50.700
So the request that
were being served

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:52.200
by the previous
version of release

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:54.450
are being completed
before a new one is

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:58.977
being sent to the user in
response to a HTTP request.

00:22:58.977 --> 00:23:00.060
DONNA MALAYERI: All right.

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:01.810
Mark, I think you
should make this better.

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:04.740
So I noticed that when you
are in the Cloud Build UI,

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:07.210
you are refreshing to see what
the status is .Can you have

00:23:07.210 --> 00:23:08.250
this trigger?

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:09.000
MARK CHMARNY: Yes.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:11.430
So as part of our
demo again, we're

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:15.180
going to do kind of
an add-on process.

00:23:15.180 --> 00:23:20.010
We're going to go in and use
one of the features of Cloud

00:23:20.010 --> 00:23:24.830
Build which publishes build
status changes into it

00:23:24.830 --> 00:23:26.100
pub/sub topic.

00:23:26.100 --> 00:23:27.750
Pub/sub up is the
globally-distributed,

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:33.130
simple-to-use, elastic message
queue service offered by GCP.

00:23:33.130 --> 00:23:36.420
And so there is--
whenever you have a Cloud

00:23:36.420 --> 00:23:38.620
Build in your
project configured,

00:23:38.620 --> 00:23:40.950
it will automatically
create that topic for you,

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:42.660
so you don't have to
do anything there.

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:44.077
What we're going
to do is we going

00:23:44.077 --> 00:23:46.350
to create a very simple
subscription that

